I give up. I did not change `/work/putnam_2011_a5.lean`, so it still contains the original `sorry`.

I read the file and worked out a mathematical proof, but I don't think I can turn it into a Lean proof reliably in this session.

The idea: let G be an antiderivative of g, so G is strictly increasing with G' = g. The hypothesis on ∇F gives F_u/g(u) + F_v/g(v) = 0 everywhere, so F(u,v) = h(G(u) − G(v)) for some function h with h(0) = 0. The bound x²g(x) ≤ 1 makes G(ℝ) an interval of finite length L. Sorting the n+1 values G(x_i) gives two with G-gap at most L/n. If h is Lipschitz near 0 with constant K, then the minimum is at most KL/n, so C = KL works.

The Lean side is the hard part. It needs:
- the inverse of G and its derivative, or an equivalent invariance argument, to show F is constant along (1,1) in G-coordinates;
- the chain rule through `deriv` and `hFgrad`, with the case split on `prop3`;
- a Lipschitz bound on h from C² regularity, with uniform control for small gaps;
- the pigeonhole step over `Fin (n+1)`, and the `sInf` manipulation for a finite nonempty set.

Without Mathlib source to check lemma names, I would expect this to take several hundred lines and many compile iterations.
